<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Surasena</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 350 BCE (approx.) · capital Mathura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The ancient kingdom of Mathura on the Yamuna, early home of the cult of Krishna and a junction of northern trade routes.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Surasena emerged around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> river, a region linked in tradition with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadava</a> clans. By the 6th century BCE Buddhist sources counted it among the sixteen mahajanapadas. Its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avantiputra">Avantiputra</a>, whose mother was apparently a princess of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-avanti">Avanti</a>, became a follower of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Buddha</a> through the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahakaccana">Mahakaccana</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> stood where routes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> met roads to the west and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, which made it a busy commercial city. Buddhist texts describe it as dusty and inhospitable, yet it became a stronghold of the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Vasudeva-Krishna</a>. Around 300 BCE the Greek envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a> described the Sourasenoi as honouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Herakles</a>, usually taken to mean Krishna, at their cities Methora and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisobora">Cleisobora</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Surasena was absorbed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nanda-empire">Nanda Empire</a> in the 4th century BCE and then passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> went on to become a great centre of Kushan sculpture and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> pilgrimage, which it remains today.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/surasena.jpg" alt="Surasena" width="320" height="246"><figcaption>Image: Avantiputra7 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahajanapadas_(c._500_BCE).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 350 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mathura</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Avantiputra</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Vedic religion, Bhagavatism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Shurasena, Surasena Mahajanapada</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-525&amp;focus=surasena" aria-label="Surasena on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6720 1170 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Surasena lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7040 1426L7253 1492"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7253 1492h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7040 1426h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7040 1426h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mathura. <a href="/atlas?year=-525&amp;focus=surasena">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

